 `--mtp-dense FILE` (the `mtp/dense` bytes of an official NVFP4, UD-IQ1_M, UD-IQ2_XS or UD-IQ3_XXS pack) swaps in a tuned drafter layer; the converter
 records it in `meta.mtp.dense`. Output is about 122 GiB (123 GiB for the 8-bit variant). The base's vocabulary must be NVIDIA's.
 
+## Native Strata inputs (custom build)
+
+This branch can import a prepared Strata NVFP4/Q8 pack directly, retaining expert bytes and the original drafter:
+
+```sh
+python3 tools/convert/convert.py --gguf qwen-nvfp4-gptq-dense.gguf \
+    --strata-pack pack --ple-gguf ple-fp8.gguf --embd-gguf token-embd-bf16.gguf \
+    --strata-mtp mtp --draft-vocab mtp/draft_vocab.bin --out model.basalt
+```
+
+The native expert index must be v3. Gate/up may be NVFP4 or Q8_0 and down may independently be either; the converter
+checks every mixed expert's scale tail, geometry and file bounds. The PLE must be a scaled E4M3 table with a finite,
+positive `strata.ple.scale`; the embedding must match the dense model and be BF16. Hadamard Gyro experts are not supported.
+Packs record `required_engine_features`: `fp8-ple` and `mixed-nvfp4-q8`. Use this custom runtime, not stock v0.4.0.
+
+Legacy DeepSeek split GGUFs with separate Engram tables use `--engram-gguf TABLE.gguf`. The converter canonicalizes
+integer hash metadata and layer offsets, joins the contiguous table views, aliases `engram_wkv` to `engram_kv`, and
+folds the stored query/key gates into FP32 products. Expert and table payloads remain unchanged. This is separate from
+quality qualification against the original checkpoint; a readable output or kernel parity test alone does not establish it.
+
 ## Qwen3.8-27B pack
 
 The published pack comes from QUASAR's quantization-aware NVFP4 checkpoint
@@ -111,6 +131,32 @@ python3 tools/convert/convert.py --checkpoint nvidia --drafter dflash2 --out Qwe
   `<name>.scale`; its GDN and attention projections are FP8 and keep their E4M3 codes as `F8_E4M3` with each matrix's FP32
   `weight_scale` as `<name>.scale`. Everything else as above. About 22 GiB with the drafter.
 
+### Experimental NInfer v3 dense artifact import
+
+The custom converter also accepts a single-file v3 `qwen3_5_text` NInfer artifact:
+
+```sh
+python3 tools/convert/convert.py --ninfer model.ninfer --out model.basalt
+```
+
+This route accepts the explicit BF16, NVFP4 and Q8G32 layouts it validates; it does not infer unsupported codecs.
+Target NVFP4 code/scale planes and reciprocal global divisors are preserved. The Q8G32 output head is losslessly
+repacked as Q8_0. BF16 attention projections remain BF16, using a shared cuBLAS product with FP32 accumulation.
+The embedding is materialized as its represented BF16 lookup values. GDN channel permutations and norm offsets
+follow the target GGUF representation. Source tensors are never modified.
+
+An included DFlash2 component is imported automatically. Its NVFP4 projection weights remain NVFP4, including
+the feature projection split along target-layer columns. The small proposal selector tables are materialized
+as BF16 represented values for Basalt's selector; this is a proposal-only precision change, not a change to target
+weights. The source mask token, layer list and window size are retained. Vision and source MTP components are not
+imported by this route. Packs advertise `qwen35-bf16-projections` and, when applicable, `qwen35-nvfp4-dflash2` as
+required engine features. They require this custom runtime. The current Basalt dense path uses INT8 or FP16 KV,
+so an NVFP4 source artifact does not imply NVFP4 attention-cache storage in Basalt.
+
+Focused tests cover import mappings, exact codes/scales, head pairing, BF16 products and a real DFlash2 serving
+smoke on Linux SM120. These checks do not establish BF16-reference perplexity, vision support or sustained
+throughput. Compare complete workload receipts before choosing a profile.
+
 ### `NVFP4_PLANES` (tensor type 45)
 
 An NVFP4 matrix of `rows` x `K` (rows a multiple of 128, K of 64) as two planes in one tensor, 36 bytes per 64 values like
